1
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:03,635
Welcome to CLM crew training.

2
00:00:03,635 --> 00:00:11,212
In about five minutes you'll learn to start the service, ask it questions, and train a classifier on your own labelled examples.

3
00:00:11,212 --> 00:00:15,651
Then you'll see how to judge whether that classifier is good enough to use.

4
00:00:17,341 --> 00:00:19,095
CLM is a fast classifier.

5
00:00:19,095 --> 00:00:24,163
A frozen NVIDIA Nemotron model reads each text once and turns it into numbers.

6
00:00:24,163 --> 00:00:27,022
Two small networks on top learn your labels.

7
00:00:27,022 --> 00:00:34,169
Training takes seconds to minutes, answers come back in tens of milliseconds, and nothing leaves your machine.

8
00:00:34,169 --> 00:00:39,951
Use it for routing, tagging and triage, where the labels are fixed and you have examples.

9
00:00:39,951 --> 00:00:44,239
For open questions or writing, use a large language model instead.

10
00:00:45,839 --> 00:00:51,888
To start it, clone the repository, open Claude Code in the folder, and type: start the service.

11
00:00:51,888 --> 00:00:57,683
The first time, it installs everything and downloads the models, which takes a few minutes.

12
00:00:57,683 --> 00:01:04,178
After that it starts in seconds, and prints the address to open: localhost:8700.

13
00:01:05,746 --> 00:01:07,106
This is the playground.

14
00:01:07,106 --> 00:01:16,094
At the top: the switch between the playground and training, and the status light, which shows that the service is ready, and how many models are loaded.

15
00:01:16,094 --> 00:01:18,933
Hover over any question mark for an explanation.

16
00:01:20,286 --> 00:01:23,738
On the left is the state: the text you want to classify.

17
00:01:23,738 --> 00:01:25,402
Below it are the questions.

18
00:01:25,402 --> 00:01:30,025
A yes or no question, a pick-one question with options, and a rating scale.

19
00:01:30,025 --> 00:01:34,339
Choose a model, here the shipped typed decisions model, and press Run.

20
00:01:35,758 --> 00:01:37,811
Each question gets its own chart.

21
00:01:37,811 --> 00:01:42,850
The highlighted bar is the model's answer, and the percentage is its probability.

22
00:01:42,850 --> 00:01:48,263
Treat those numbers as a ranking, not a guarantee, until the model has been calibrated.

23
00:01:48,263 --> 00:01:54,796
The JSON tab shows exactly what came back, and the Python tab gives a developer the same request as code.

24
00:01:56,110 --> 00:01:59,527
Now the main event: training your own classifier.

25
00:01:59,527 --> 00:02:01,200
Switch to Train a model.

26
00:02:01,200 --> 00:02:07,335
Four steps on one page: add examples, describe the labels, train, and check the results.

27
00:02:08,743 --> 00:02:09,779
First, the data.

28
00:02:09,779 --> 00:02:14,832
You need a file with one column of text and one column with the correct label.

29
00:02:14,832 --> 00:02:17,099
A spreadsheet saved as CSV works.

30
00:02:17,099 --> 00:02:19,884
Aim for at least thirty examples per label.

31
00:02:19,884 --> 00:02:27,397
If several rows come from the same customer or case, include that ID column: you'll use it to keep the test honest.

32
00:02:29,002 --> 00:02:35,357
We'll use the bundled sample: four hundred synthetic support conversations, in five categories.

33
00:02:35,357 --> 00:02:41,043
CLM finds the text and label columns by itself, and highlights them in the preview.

34
00:02:41,043 --> 00:02:44,254
The chips show how many examples each label has.

35
00:02:44,254 --> 00:02:47,331
A label with very few examples gets a warning.

36
00:02:48,744 --> 00:02:50,088
Step two matters most.

37
00:02:50,088 --> 00:02:52,532
The model doesn't read your label names.

38
00:02:52,532 --> 00:02:56,502
It reads your descriptions, and compares every text against them.

39
00:02:56,502 --> 00:03:01,084
So describe each label with the words that actually appear in its examples.

40
00:03:01,084 --> 00:03:05,665
Watch what happens with a weak description: the page warns you as you type.

41
00:03:05,665 --> 00:03:07,376
Let's put the good one back.

42
00:03:08,818 --> 00:03:09,518
Step three.

43
00:03:09,518 --> 00:03:16,198
Give the model a name, check how the data is split, here the file's own test rows, and press Train model.

44
00:03:16,198 --> 00:03:20,969
The encoder reads every text once, then the small networks train in rounds.

45
00:03:20,969 --> 00:03:25,168
The line shows accuracy on a check set the model doesn't train on.

46
00:03:25,168 --> 00:03:28,603
The dashed line is where it started, without training.

47
00:03:28,603 --> 00:03:31,720
Training stops by itself when it stops improving.

48
00:03:32,870 --> 00:03:34,536
Step four: is it any good?

49
00:03:34,536 --> 00:03:38,446
This model gets most of a hundred unseen conversations right.

50
00:03:38,446 --> 00:03:46,650
Compare that with the two baselines below it: the base model without training, and simply always guessing the most common label.

51
00:03:46,650 --> 00:03:49,086
A useful model must clearly beat both.

52
00:03:49,086 --> 00:03:54,854
And mind the margin: with a hundred test examples, a few points either way is just chance.

53
00:03:56,132 --> 00:03:59,030
Try it on a new message before you trust it.

54
00:03:59,030 --> 00:04:01,863
Type or paste any text, and press Classify.

55
00:04:01,863 --> 00:04:04,695
Here, a double charge is routed to billing.

56
00:04:06,041 --> 00:04:08,691
The model is already live on this server.

57
00:04:08,691 --> 00:04:12,504
The copy buttons give developers ready-made Python or curl.

58
00:04:12,504 --> 00:04:16,834
Open in playground loads the model with its question, ready to use.

59
00:04:16,834 --> 00:04:20,001
And it's saved, so it comes back after a restart.

60
00:04:21,470 --> 00:04:24,276
With your own data, a few habits matter most.

61
00:04:24,276 --> 00:04:26,521
Use labels that people have checked.

62
00:04:26,521 --> 00:04:29,577
Group rows by customer, so the test stays honest.

63
00:04:29,577 --> 00:04:34,877
Describe each label with the words its examples use, and avoid catch-alls like other.

64
00:04:34,877 --> 00:04:41,612
If the model doesn't clearly beat both baselines, improve the descriptions or the data before anything else.

65
00:04:41,612 --> 00:04:44,107
And retrain whenever your labels change.

66
00:04:45,715 --> 00:04:47,003
Finally, governance.

67
00:04:47,003 --> 00:04:51,897
The models shipped with the repository are demos, trained on synthetic data.

68
00:04:51,897 --> 00:05:01,685
Before a model makes real decisions, it needs human-labelled data, calibrated probabilities, and the new model checklist in the Model Governance folder.

69
00:05:01,685 --> 00:05:05,613
The user guide covers everything in this video, step by step.

70
00:05:05,613 --> 00:05:06,900
Thanks for watching.
